  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1218 PM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nHigh pressure centered to our southeast, and the resultant\nsouthwesterly flow over our area, continues to advect monsoonal\nmoisture into our CWA. Storm motion is forecast to remain slow \nwhile ridging is aloft. At the same time, this mornings sounding\nshows that precipitable water remains well above normal for \nthis time of the year, and there is an increase in instability \nwhen compared to yesterday. So, expect another day of slow \nmoving storms capable of producing heavy rainfall. This means \nflooding and debris flow concerns remain elevated, especially \nover our recent burn scars. Lightning, small hail, and strong \noutflow winds are also concerns with thunderstorms. \n\nHighs remain anywhere from 5 to 10 degrees above climatological\nnorms with 100s still in the forecast for many lower elevations.\nOvernight lows will drop, obviously, but still remain above \nnormal and without much relief, the going Heat Advisory remains \nin effect for the Grand Valley and Lower Yampa River Basin \nthrough tomorrow morning. \n\nLooking ahead, a shortwave looks to move across the area \nWednesday. If this should occur, thunderstorm chances will \nbecome more widespread, and potential for some stronger \nthunderstorms would increase. Keep an eye on the latest \nforecast. After that, high pressure continues to be forecast to \nbuild in to our west. Flow around the high will cause moisture \nto slowly decrease which will temper convection across the \nregion.",
